## Key Analytical Notes & Data Integrity Flags

**Ratio disclosure structure:** All ratios in Note 3.27 "Analytical Ratios" are segmented by product line (Health vs. Personal Accident). No blended company-wide percentage is separately published; the Health-segment figures — representing ~99% of the total book — have been used as reported above. A cross-check using raw totals (Total Claims Incurred ÷ Total NEP = ₹93.07 cr / ₹1,459.24 cr) computes to **63.78%**, closely aligning with the disclosed 64% Health-segment figure and confirming internal consistency.

**Retail Health GWP — not separable:** The Annual Report's product-wise business table provides only a blended "Retail Total (A) = ₹754.90 cr" figure combining Health products (ProHealth Insurance, Prime, Lifetime Health, Prime Senior, Super Top Up, Select, Cash) with non-health retail lines (Lifestyle Protection Accident/Critical Care, Accident Shield, Saral Suraksha Bima). No standalone "Retail Health only" GWP subtotal is published, so this line has been left blank rather than estimated.

**IFRS basis unavailable:** ManipalCigna reports exclusively under Indian GAAP per IRDAI (Preparation of Financial Statements) Regulations, 2002. No IFRS-basis financials or ratios exist for this insurer.

**Market share not disclosed:** Retail health market share is not published anywhere in the FY23-24 Annual Report — there is no IRDAI/GI Council benchmarking table included in this filing.

**PAT trend:** The FY24 net loss of **₹(131.82) cr** represents a narrowing of losses versus FY23's **₹(200.93) cr**, driven primarily by a lower Expense-of-Management (EoM) excess transfer (₹173.43 cr vs. ₹221.54 cr in FY23), despite commission costs nearly tripling (from ₹13.85 cr to ₹34.34 cr). This reflects a regulatory-driven shift in cost mix from operating expenses to commission under the EoM glide-path compliance framework.

**Solvency position:** The **1.66x** solvency ratio comfortably exceeds the IRDAI-mandated minimum of 1.50x, though it has only marginally improved from 1.56x in FY23. This reflects continued capital support — including a ₹27.62 cr share capital infusion during the year — needed to offset the accumulated P&L debit balance of ₹1,593.86 cr.</ans>
